1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,236
High on its hill, the kingdom of Vessel-on-the-Hill wraps a great stone wall around a red river.

2
00:00:04,636 --> 00:00:09,636
On peaceful days the wall's lanterns glow softly, and the message to everyone is simple: keep flowing.

3
00:00:10,486 --> 00:00:11,591
Then, crack.

4
00:00:11,991 --> 00:00:13,467
A siege stone splits the wall,

5
00:00:13,587 --> 00:00:15,108
and the river begins to spill.

6
00:00:15,958 --> 00:00:18,467
What happens next always happens in the same order.

7
00:00:18,867 --> 00:00:19,898
The walls squeeze.

8
00:00:20,298 --> 00:00:21,408
The Guards rush in.

9
00:00:21,808 --> 00:00:24,308
A torch relay races along the castle road to wake the Mayor,

10
00:00:24,427 --> 00:00:25,462
who spins a net.

11
00:00:25,862 --> 00:00:28,998
And when the wall has healed, a demolition crew clears the net away.

12
00:00:29,398 --> 00:00:31,234
This is the story of the day the wall tore.

13
00:00:32,084 --> 00:00:32,984
Module one.

14
00:00:33,384 --> 00:00:34,473
The Day the Wall Tore.

15
00:00:35,323 --> 00:00:40,808
This is haemostasis: the body's way of stopping bleeding at a site of injury, while keeping blood fluid everywhere else.

16
00:00:41,208 --> 00:00:43,228
Remember both halves of that sentence.

17
00:00:43,628 --> 00:00:45,620
Haemostasis is not just clotting.

18
00:00:46,020 --> 00:00:49,076
It is clotting in the right place, for the right length of time.

19
00:00:49,476 --> 00:00:51,479
We describe four phases: vascular,

20
00:00:51,599 --> 00:00:52,275
platelet,

21
00:00:52,395 --> 00:00:53,451
coagulation

22
00:00:53,851 --> 00:00:55,019
and fibrinolytic.

23
00:00:55,419 --> 00:00:59,193
The vascular and platelet phases together are called primary haemostasis.

24
00:00:59,593 --> 00:01:01,797
Coagulation is secondary haemostasis.

25
00:01:02,647 --> 00:01:04,544
Stop the bleed, keep the flow.

26
00:01:05,694 --> 00:01:09,005
Before any tear, the wall itself is the kingdom's chief peacekeeper.

27
00:01:09,405 --> 00:01:11,962
Intact endothelium is actively antithrombotic.

28
00:01:12,362 --> 00:01:17,817
It releases prostacyclin and nitric oxide, which dilate the vessel and keep platelets quiet.

29
00:01:18,217 --> 00:01:22,402
Its surface carries heparan sulphate, which strongly accelerates antithrombin,

30
00:01:22,522 --> 00:01:27,634
and thrombomodulin, which captures thrombin and redirects it to activate protein C.

31
00:01:28,034 --> 00:01:34,521
It expresses tissue factor pathway inhibitor, and it breaks down the platelet agonist ADP using the surface enzyme CD39,

32
00:01:34,641 --> 00:01:37,216
and it secretes tissue plasminogen activator.

33
00:01:38,066 --> 00:01:44,607
The molecules that start clotting, collagen and tissue factor, sit behind the endothelium, out of contact with flowing blood.

34
00:01:45,457 --> 00:01:47,117
A healthy wall keeps the peace.

35
00:01:48,267 --> 00:01:49,531
The stone strikes.

36
00:01:49,931 --> 00:01:52,550
The wall flinches and squeezes, narrowing the gap.

37
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:54,939
The vascular phase is immediate.

38
00:01:55,339 --> 00:01:59,088
A local reflex causes vasoconstriction, which slows blood flow.

39
00:01:59,488 --> 00:02:05,546
It is reinforced by thromboxane A2 and serotonin released from platelets, and by endothelin from the endothelium.

40
00:02:05,946 --> 00:02:08,516
Vasoconstriction matters most in small vessels.

41
00:02:09,366 --> 00:02:12,971
Behind the broken stones, an alarm bell is suddenly exposed to the river.

42
00:02:13,371 --> 00:02:16,593
Injury also exposes what was hidden: subendothelial collagen,

43
00:02:16,713 --> 00:02:18,995
von Willebrand factor bound to the matrix,

44
00:02:19,115 --> 00:02:22,950
and tissue factor on cells such as fibroblasts and smooth muscle cells.

45
00:02:23,350 --> 00:02:25,771
Tissue factor is the trigger for coagulation.

46
00:02:26,171 --> 00:02:28,580
Note that these events start at the same moment.

47
00:02:28,980 --> 00:02:32,877
The four phases overlap, and we separate them only to make them easier to understand.

48
00:02:33,727 --> 00:02:35,263
First, the walls squeeze.

49
00:02:35,963 --> 00:02:38,338
Tissue factor is the alarm bell behind the wall.

50
00:02:39,488 --> 00:02:40,926
The Platelet Guards arrive.

51
00:02:41,326 --> 00:02:43,448
They throw ropes to the wall and tie on.

52
00:02:43,848 --> 00:02:46,805
Then they raise spiked shields and link arms with one another.

53
00:02:47,205 --> 00:02:50,321
The gap is plugged, but a line of guards can still be pushed aside.

54
00:02:51,171 --> 00:02:53,389
Primary haemostasis has three steps.

55
00:02:53,789 --> 00:02:54,544
Adhesion.

56
00:02:54,944 --> 00:02:59,128
Under the high shear of arterial and small-vessel flow, platelet glycoprotein Ib,

57
00:02:59,248 --> 00:03:05,237
part of the GPIb-IX-V complex, binds von Willebrand factor attached to exposed collagen.

58
00:03:05,637 --> 00:03:10,400
Platelets also bind collagen directly through GPVI and integrin α2β1.

59
00:03:11,250 --> 00:03:12,223
Activation.

60
00:03:12,623 --> 00:03:14,049
Platelets change shape,

61
00:03:14,169 --> 00:03:18,250
release granule contents such as ADP, and make thromboxane A2.

62
00:03:18,650 --> 00:03:20,329
These recruit more platelets.

63
00:03:20,729 --> 00:03:25,566
The activated platelet also flips negatively charged phosphatidylserine to its outer surface.

64
00:03:25,966 --> 00:03:29,031
This surface is the stage on which coagulation will be built.

65
00:03:29,881 --> 00:03:30,797
Aggregation.

66
00:03:31,197 --> 00:03:35,451
Activated glycoprotein IIb/IIIa binds fibrinogen and von Willebrand factor,

67
00:03:35,571 --> 00:03:37,631
which bridge one platelet to the next.

68
00:03:38,031 --> 00:03:44,337
The Rope has one more job: in plasma, von Willebrand factor carries factor VIII and protects it from rapid clearance.

69
00:03:44,737 --> 00:03:47,956
The platelet plug forms within minutes, but it is fragile.

70
00:03:48,356 --> 00:03:50,614
It needs a fibrin net to hold it in place.

71
00:03:51,464 --> 00:03:53,019
Rope ties guards to the wall.

72
00:03:53,719 --> 00:03:56,901
GPIb grabs the rope; IIb/IIIa links guard to guard.

73
00:03:57,601 --> 00:04:00,016
Guards plug the gap: quick but fragile.

74
00:04:01,166 --> 00:04:03,852
At the alarm bell, the first messenger lights a torch.

75
00:04:04,252 --> 00:04:07,747
Each messenger lights the next, and the flame runs along the castle road.

76
00:04:08,147 --> 00:04:12,151
But a messenger can only stand on the guards' shields if it is painted with magic ink.

77
00:04:12,551 --> 00:04:14,425
The messengers are built in the Liver Workshop,

78
00:04:14,545 --> 00:04:20,929
but four of them, II, VII, IX and X, cannot grip the ink until the Vitamin K Forge has put the final edge on them.

79
00:04:21,329 --> 00:04:23,518
At the end of the road waits Thrombin the Mayor.

80
00:04:24,368 --> 00:04:27,787
Coagulation turns the fragile platelet plug into a stable clot.

81
00:04:28,187 --> 00:04:31,696
Most clotting factors circulate as inactive precursors of enzymes.

82
00:04:32,096 --> 00:04:36,809
Each activated factor activates the next, so a small signal is amplified into a large burst.

83
00:04:37,659 --> 00:04:41,268
In the body, coagulation starts when tissue factor binds factor VIIa.

84
00:04:41,668 --> 00:04:46,491
This complex activates factors X and IX, and a small amount of thrombin forms.

85
00:04:46,891 --> 00:04:48,074
This is initiation.

86
00:04:48,474 --> 00:04:51,413
That small amount of thrombin then amplifies the process.

87
00:04:51,813 --> 00:04:58,785
It fully activates platelets, and it activates factors V, VIII and XI, releasing VIII from its von Willebrand factor carrier.

88
00:04:59,185 --> 00:05:03,281
In propagation, two complexes assemble on the activated platelet surface.

89
00:05:03,681 --> 00:05:07,316
The tenase complex, IXa with VIIIa, activates factor X.

90
00:05:07,716 --> 00:05:12,441
The prothrombinase complex, Xa with Va, converts prothrombin to thrombin.

91
00:05:12,841 --> 00:05:14,628
The result is a burst of thrombin.

92
00:05:15,478 --> 00:05:20,218
These complexes need calcium, which bridges the factors to the negatively charged phospholipid.

93
00:05:20,618 --> 00:05:25,036
That is why blood taken into a citrate tube does not clot: citrate binds calcium,

94
00:05:25,156 --> 00:05:27,925
and the laboratory adds calcium back to start the test.

95
00:05:28,775 --> 00:05:35,244
Factors II, VII, IX and X, together with the natural anticoagulants protein C and protein S, are made in the liver,

96
00:05:35,364 --> 00:05:38,943
but they only become functional once vitamin K has completed them.

97
00:05:39,343 --> 00:05:44,399
Vitamin K is the cofactor for gamma-carboxylation, which gives them the calcium-binding Gla domains.

98
00:05:44,799 --> 00:05:47,080
Warfarin blocks the recycling of vitamin K,

99
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:51,923
so it takes days to work fully: the factors already in the circulation have to be cleared first.

100
00:05:52,773 --> 00:05:55,995
Finally, thrombin converts soluble fibrinogen into fibrin,

101
00:05:56,115 --> 00:06:00,340
and activates factor XIII, which cross-links the fibrin into a stable mesh.

102
00:06:01,190 --> 00:06:03,866
Each torch lights the next, and the flame grows.

103
00:06:04,566 --> 00:06:05,830
No ink, no stick.

104
00:06:06,530 --> 00:06:09,254
Ten, nine, seven, two: finished by K.

105
00:06:09,954 --> 00:06:11,491
The Mayor turns thread into net.

106
00:06:12,641 --> 00:06:15,450
A relay that never stopped would set the whole kingdom ablaze.

107
00:06:15,850 --> 00:06:18,716
So at every gate stands a Gatekeeper, dressed in gold.

108
00:06:19,566 --> 00:06:22,157
Natural anticoagulants keep the clot local.

109
00:06:22,557 --> 00:06:28,238
Antithrombin inactivates thrombin and factor Xa, and to a lesser extent IXa, XIa and XIIa.

110
00:06:28,638 --> 00:06:32,533
Heparin, and heparan sulphate on the vessel wall, accelerate it enormously.

111
00:06:33,383 --> 00:06:37,691
Protein C is activated when thrombin binds thrombomodulin on healthy endothelium.

112
00:06:38,091 --> 00:06:41,316
So once the Mayor steps outside the breach, he changes sides.

113
00:06:41,716 --> 00:06:47,216
Activated protein C, with its cofactor protein S, cleaves and inactivates factors Va and VIIIa.

114
00:06:47,616 --> 00:06:51,342
Tissue factor pathway inhibitor switches off the starting complex early on.

115
00:06:51,742 --> 00:06:55,388
When a Gatekeeper is missing, the result is thrombosis, not bleeding.

116
00:06:56,238 --> 00:06:59,252
Antithrombin locks the Mayor's door; heparin speeds the key.

117
00:06:59,952 --> 00:07:02,783
C cuts, S supports: Five and Eight stop.

118
00:07:03,933 --> 00:07:06,667
When the wall is mended, Foreman tPA blows his whistle.

119
00:07:07,067 --> 00:07:11,307
The Demolition Crew, who have been camped beside the net all along, take it apart

120
00:07:11,707 --> 00:07:13,258
and leave a little rubble behind.

121
00:07:14,108 --> 00:07:16,548
Fibrinolysis removes the clot as the vessel heals.

122
00:07:16,948 --> 00:07:22,085
Tissue plasminogen activator, released from endothelium, converts plasminogen into plasmin.

123
00:07:22,485 --> 00:07:26,412
It works far better when both are bound to fibrin, which keeps lysis local.

124
00:07:26,812 --> 00:07:30,134
Plasmin breaks fibrin into fibrin degradation products.

125
00:07:30,534 --> 00:07:36,997
Cross-linked fibrin, made by factor XIII, breaks down into fragments containing two linked D-domains: D-dimer.

126
00:07:37,397 --> 00:07:40,968
A raised D-dimer tells us that fibrin was formed and then broken down.

127
00:07:41,818 --> 00:07:43,274
The crew has brakes too.

128
00:07:43,674 --> 00:07:45,110
PAI-1 inhibits tPA,

129
00:07:45,230 --> 00:07:47,701
and α2-antiplasmin inhibits plasmin.

130
00:07:48,101 --> 00:07:50,730
Formation and breakdown of fibrin overlap in time.

131
00:07:51,130 --> 00:07:53,302
The crew's tools are on site from the start.

132
00:07:54,152 --> 00:07:55,922
Build the net, then clear the net.

133
00:07:56,622 --> 00:07:58,574
D-dimer is rubble from a stitched net.

134
00:07:59,724 --> 00:08:03,780
When something goes wrong, the kingdom sends its Inspectors, dressed in grey.

135
00:08:04,630 --> 00:08:07,872
In the laboratory we split coagulation into two roads that join.

136
00:08:08,272 --> 00:08:11,732
The prothrombin time, PT, is started with tissue factor.

137
00:08:12,132 --> 00:08:18,456
It tests the short, extrinsic road through factor VII, then the common road: X, V, II and fibrinogen.

138
00:08:18,856 --> 00:08:22,203
The INR is derived from the PT to standardise warfarin monitoring.

139
00:08:22,603 --> 00:08:26,588
The activated partial thromboplastin time, APTT, tests the long,

140
00:08:26,708 --> 00:08:30,975
intrinsic road, XII, XI, IX and VIII, and then the same common road.

141
00:08:31,375 --> 00:08:35,342
The thrombin time tests only the final step, from fibrinogen to fibrin.

142
00:08:35,742 --> 00:08:37,360
These roads are a laboratory model.

143
00:08:37,760 --> 00:08:43,324
In the body, factor XII is not needed: its deficiency prolongs the APTT but does not cause bleeding.

144
00:08:44,174 --> 00:08:48,154
Worked example: in haemophilia A, messenger VIII is missing from the long road.

145
00:08:48,554 --> 00:08:50,917
The APTT is prolonged; the PT is normal.

146
00:08:51,767 --> 00:08:53,758
PT: short road, Seven.

147
00:08:54,458 --> 00:08:57,896
APTT: long road: Twelve, Eleven, Nine, Eight.

148
00:08:58,596 --> 00:09:00,285
TT watches the Mayor's last job.

149
00:09:00,985 --> 00:09:04,061
Eight missing on the long road: APTT long, PT normal.

150
00:09:05,211 --> 00:09:07,001
Let's walk the wall one more time.

151
00:09:07,401 --> 00:09:09,298
Stop the bleed, keep the flow.

152
00:09:09,698 --> 00:09:11,357
A healthy wall keeps the peace.

153
00:09:11,757 --> 00:09:14,428
First, the walls squeeze, and the alarm bell rings.

154
00:09:14,828 --> 00:09:16,384
Rope ties guards to the wall.

155
00:09:16,784 --> 00:09:19,198
Guards plug the gap: quick but fragile.

156
00:09:19,598 --> 00:09:21,238
Each torch lights the next.

157
00:09:21,638 --> 00:09:22,902
No ink, no stick.

158
00:09:23,302 --> 00:09:26,027
Ten, nine, seven, two: finished by K.

159
00:09:26,427 --> 00:09:27,963
The Mayor turns thread into net.

160
00:09:28,363 --> 00:09:30,046
C cuts, S supports.

161
00:09:30,446 --> 00:09:32,216
Build the net, then clear the net.

162
00:09:32,616 --> 00:09:36,747
And in haemophilia A: Eight missing on the long road, APTT long, PT normal.

163
00:09:37,597 --> 00:09:40,520
Next time: the Platelet Guards, up close.
